      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C44", "C45", "C46", "C47", "C48", "C49", "C50", "C51", "C52", "C53", "C54", "C55"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself in the supplied records.",
    "No L4 intestinal absorption, solubility, or permeability record concerns the query or a close structural analog; the selected records are for unrelated molecules.",
    "Azosemide, the closest structural analog, was studied in fasted healthy subjects rather than cirrhosis patients and has conflicting estimates (20.4% L1 absolute versus 10% L2 urinary recovery).",
    "Cirrhosis effects on the query-specific metabolic and transporter pathways are not directly measured.",
    "The query's furan, carboxylic acid, and chloro substituents may alter first-pass metabolism and transporter handling relative to bumetanide and azosemide."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L4 intestinal absorption/permeability records are for unrelated molecules and do not provide query-specific evidence, so the prior decision is retained. The prediction remains high based on condition-matched high-F analogs: bumetanide 95% in cirrhosis, metoclopramide 79-82%, tolfenamic acid about 100%, and the closest structural analog azosemide at 20.4% L1 absolute F. The indirect azosemide urinary-recovery estimate of 10% is outweighed by the L1 absolute value and the condition-matched analog evidence."
}

